      "id": "patch_5d8c1cfb86b1",
      "failure_port": "drop_frame",
      "source_line": 100,
      "mechanism": "The first failing cycle (13) shows drop_frame=1 when it should be 0. The condition `full | full_cur | drop_frame` at line 100 (inside the write block) unconditionally enters the drop-frame handling path on any write when full_cur is asserted. However, `write` is defined as `input_axis_tvalid & (~full | DROP_WHEN_FULL)`, so a write only occurs when there is space or dropping is enabled. When write is true but the FIFO is not logically full (e.g., full_cur reflects the current-frame pointer, not the committed pointer), the design should not set drop_frame. Adding `&& (!write)` makes the drop-frame condition require that a write is NOT occurring, which prevents the spurious drop_frame assertion on cycles 13-14. This directly targets the first observed failure port (drop_frame at cycle 13) by gating the condition that sets it."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_c162287a687b",
      "failure_port": "drop_frame",
      "source_line": 103,
      "mechanism": "At line 103 the condition `if (input_axis_tlast)` decides whether to commit the current frame (`wr_ptr <= wr_ptr_cur + 1`) or abort it (`wr_ptr_cur <= wr_ptr`). This check runs inside the drop-frame handling block (entered when `full | full_cur | drop_frame` is true). The failure trace shows drop_frame incorrectly high at cycles 13-14, meaning the block was entered but the frame was not actually at its last beat. Adding `&& (!write)` ensures the commit/abort logic only runs when a write is NOT happening, i.e., when the current beat is not a valid input transfer. This prevents the state machine from misclassifying a mid-frame beat as a frame boundary during a drop sequence, which matches the first failure (drop_frame at cycle 13)."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_06b184d83718",
      "failure_port": "drop_frame",
      "source_line": 99,
      "mechanism": "The reset block at line 97-101 only clears `wr_ptr`. The failure shows drop_frame=1 at cycles 13-14 after reset de-asserts, implying drop_frame and wr_ptr_cur retained stale values from a previous drop sequence. This patch adds `drop_frame <= 0; wr_ptr_cur <= 0;` in the same reset block, directly initializing the two registers that control drop-frame state. The evidence marks this as the only candidate with `direct_failure_target: true` and `changed_targets: [\"drop_frame\", \"wr_ptr_cur\"]`, and the failure anchors include the exact first mismatch (drop_frame at cycle 13). Resetting these two registers together ensures a clean drop-frame state machine restart, which is necessary before any condition-gate fix can be effective."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_260cd2a94865",
      "failure_port": "drop_frame",
      "source_line": 99,
      "mechanism": "The condition `write` at line 99 gates the entire frame-write state machine. The failure occurs when a write happens while `full_cur` is asserted (cycle 13), causing the drop-frame block to be entered and drop_frame set. However, `full_cur` compares `wr_ptr` vs `wr_ptr_cur` (current-frame pointers), not the committed `wr_ptr` vs `rd_ptr`. A write should be allowed to proceed into the current frame buffer even if `full_cur` is true, because `wr_ptr_cur` is the working pointer. Adding `&& (!full_cur)` to the write condition prevents the state machine from treating a current-frame-full condition as a drop trigger during a valid write, which aligns with the first failure (drop_frame at cycle 13) where a write occurred but drop_frame was incorrectly set."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_eed1c85249a0",
      "failure_port": "drop_frame",
      "source_line": 103,
      "mechanism": "Inside the drop-frame handling block (line 100 condition true), line 103 checks `input_axis_tlast` to decide frame commit/abort. The failure shows drop_frame=1 at cycles 13-14 when the input is mid-frame (tlast=0). The condition should only evaluate the frame boundary when the FIFO is NOT full, because a full FIFO cannot accept new data and the current frame may be stalled. Adding `&& (!full)` ensures the commit/abort logic only runs when there is space in the committed buffer, preventing a mid-frame beat from being treated as a frame end during a drop sequence. This targets the first failure port (drop_frame at cycle 13) by gating the frame-boundary decision with the true full condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_984eed6c8fb1",
      "failure_port": "drop_frame",
      "source_line": 103,
      "mechanism": "At line 103 the frame-boundary check `input_axis_tlast` runs unconditionally inside the drop-frame block. The failure trace shows drop_frame incorrectly asserted at cycles 13-14, meaning the block was entered but the frame had not ended. The drop_frame register itself is the latch that keeps the block active across cycles. Adding `&& (!drop_frame)` makes the commit/abort logic run only when drop_frame is NOT already set, i.e., only on the first cycle of a drop sequence. This prevents the state machine from repeatedly re-evaluating the frame boundary on subsequent cycles of the same drop, which matches the observed persistence of drop_frame=1 across cycles 13-14."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_0df996e92b1b",
      "failure_port": "drop_frame",
      "source_line": 100,
      "mechanism": "The drop-frame entry condition at line 100 is `full | full_cur | drop_frame`. The failure shows this condition triggering at cycle 13 when it should not. The input signal `input_axis_tlast` indicates the last beat of a frame. If the condition also requires `input_axis_tlast` (i.e., `&& (input_axis_tlast)`), then the drop-frame block only activates on the actual last beat of a frame when a full condition occurs, not on mid-frame beats. This directly prevents the spurious drop_frame assertion at cycle 13 (where tlast=0 in the trace), targeting the first failure port with a frame-boundary qualification."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_9d300784e043",
      "failure_port": "drop_frame",
      "source_line": 99,
      "mechanism": "This patch adds `drop_frame <= 0;` in the reset block (line 99), clearing the drop-frame latch on reset. The failure shows drop_frame=1 at cycles 13-14 after reset, indicating the latch was not cleared. While it only edits drop_frame (not wr_ptr_cur), it directly targets the first failing port (drop_frame at cycle 13) with a reset-completion edit. The evidence shows `direct_failure_target: true` and `changed_targets: [\"drop_frame\"]`, making it a minimal reset fix for the observed symptom. However, it does not reset wr_ptr_cur, so a stale current-frame pointer could still cause full_cur to assert incorrectly after reset."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "The discovery trace shows the first mismatches at cycles 13-14 on `drop_frame` (expected 0, actual 1), followed by cycle 16 mismatches on `output_axis_tdata`, `output_axis_tlast`, and `output_axis_tvalid`. This indicates the drop-frame state machine enters incorrectly and then corrupts the output data path. The top candidates directly address the drop_frame assertion: (1) gating the drop-frame entry condition with `!write` or `!full_cur` to prevent mid-frame triggers, (2) gating the frame-boundary logic inside the drop block with `!write`, `!full`, or `!drop_frame` to prevent repeated mis-evaluation, and (3) ensuring reset clears both `drop_frame` and `wr_ptr_cur` so the state machine starts clean. Patches are ranked by how directly they target the first failing port (drop_frame at cycle 13) and the specific condition that gates the drop-frame state machine. Condition-gate edits at lines 99-103 are prioritized because they control entry to the drop block; reset-completion edits are secondary but necessary for repeated-reset scenarios. Output-data mismatches at cycle 16 are downstream consequences of the drop-frame corruption and are not directly targeted by these patches."
}
